{{- /* Git hook shim for one stage (#196, #361): thin entry only, no gate
       logic. Input: (dict "root" . "stage" "<git hook name>") from the
       per-stage file in private_dot_config/git-hook-gates/hooks/.

       The dispatcher is deployed by agent-tools sync to BOTH tool homes with
       identical bytes (agent-tools#202); pointing at the ~/.claude deploy is
       the dotfiles pick of record (docs/git-hook-gates.md). $HOME is expanded
       by sh at runtime, so no literal home path is baked into the render.

       The dispatcher runs the stage's gates, if it has any (pre-commit:
       public-safety then git-identity — agent-tools#281 / #239; commit-msg:
       public-safety then ai-trailer; pre-merge-commit and
       prepare-commit-msg: git-identity only — agent-tools#449), and then
       chains to the repo's own $GIT_DIR/hooks/<stage>. Every other stage is
       a pass-through to that repo hook (agent-tools#460, GIT_HOOK_STAGES):
       global core.hooksPath replaces .git/hooks, so a stage without a shim
       silently drops the repo's own hook (Git LFS's pre-push / post-checkout
       / post-commit / post-merge, for one) (#361). Deliberately no shim
       (docs/git-hook-gates.md): push-to-checkout / proc-receive /
       fsmonitor-watchman (a hook there changes git's behaviour or must
       answer in a fixed format; the dispatcher refuses them), and
       reference-transaction / post-index-change (git runs them on every ref
       update / index write, and a dispatcher start each time made git
       operations up to ~25x slower in the #361 measurement).

       The dispatcher is FAIL-CLOSED (exit 2) when its gates are missing, so
       the shim must only render where the agent-tools deploy is COMPLETE —
       a two-key gate: enableGitHookGates (intent) AND the armed probe
       (readiness, all four scripts — dispatcher + three gates — present in
       the destination; .chezmoitemplates/git-hook-gates-armed). A fresh
       machine applies cleanly before agent-tools exists; re-apply after sync
       arms the gates. The pass-through stages need a dispatcher that knows
       them (agent-tools#460 or later); an older one refuses them with exit
       2, which stops e.g. a push loudly, so deploy agent-tools first.

       Template self-gate, NOT a module `requires`: an unmet key renders
       EMPTY and chezmoi prunes the managed target, so flipping the
       capability off (or a disappeared deploy) truly removes the shim on the
       next apply. A `requires:` gate would leave an already-applied
       fail-closed shim lingering (chezmoiignore never prunes existing
       targets — the #184 lesson, same as codex-settings). */ -}}
{{- template "require-profile" .root -}}
{{- $caps := index .root.profiles .root.profile "capabilities" -}}
{{- $armed := eq (includeTemplate "git-hook-gates-armed" (dict "destDir" .root.chezmoi.destDir)) "armed" -}}
{{- if and (index $caps "enableGitHookGates") $armed -}}
#!/bin/sh
# Managed by chezmoi from kosako/dotfiles (git-hook-gates, #196).
# Thin shim: gate logic lives in agent-tools (contract: docs/git-hook-gates.md
# there). Best-effort guardrail — a repo-local core.hooksPath bypasses it,
# --no-verify skips every gate but the git-identity one in prepare-commit-msg,
# and git am / an apply-backend rebase run no gate.
exec "$HOME/.claude/agent-tools/scripts/personal-git-hook-dispatcher" {{ .stage }} "$@"
{{ end -}}
